design is subjective  there's just no way of getting around  that fact and  architecture in a lot of ways is the art  of  convincing people that your design moves  were the right moves  and one of the ways you can convince  people is through case studies  i think of case studies like a shield if  you present it correctly  it can be a very powerful way for you to  defend your design moves  especially if you're choosing a project  that's been successful before  and it's been done by a well-known  architect it can build a very solid  foundation  for you to build your design strategies  from  and it can be a lot harder to poke holes  at something that's been  proven to be successful before there is  a lot of information out there on how to  do case studies  but not so much on how to present them  so if you're looking for information on  how to do them this is not the video for  you  in this video i'm gonna show you how to  present them in a way that's  informative in a way that's engaging  and in a way that can create a  bulletproof defense for your project  hey guys how's it going my name is dani  and i'm an architect  based in vancouver on this channel i  make videos about architecture design  and creativity  one of the reasons i started this  youtube channel is because i miss the  presentation and storytelling aspect of  architecture school  and so this is a process that i love  and it's a process that i'm always  trying to refine  but this is a fun topic for me so when i  started architecture school  i was absolutely terrible at  presentations i would get stage fright  i would get nervous i would shake i  could hear my heartbeat  and of course i would work right up to  the presentation  i would have pulled like two  all-nighters before  i would basically wing it every single  time  so as you can imagine they did not go  very well  it wasn't well into my second year of  architecture school that i  sort of started to get the hang of it i  think it was a combination  of like actually liking my project and  being proud of them and wanting to  tell a story that kind of did it justice  and also getting a little bit better at  time management so that i could  plan out my presentations beforehand so  slowly and slowly i  started to improve but it was actually  in my  thesis year when i really started to  strategize on my presentation technique  and sort of build a system around it so  this is a strategy that i've refined  over  years and years of presenting my own  work and  from observing other people present  nobody actually taught me this  so it is a little bit different from the  standard way of presenting your case  studies  but throughout my bachelor's and my  master's i did receive  numerous awards for my project and for  my thesis  i received the aia henry adams award  which is  the highest honor you can get for your  thesis project in north america  and i attribute this a lot to my  presentation methods  just before we start let's just cover  the basics what are  case studies case studies are when you  study an existing building  or an existing project usually a  well-known piece of architecture  to inform your design if you want to  design a chicken coop  you don't really want to start from  scratch you want to see how  other people have done it you want to  see how other people  located their chicken coops how they  shaped it  how they place the doors and you want to  learn from their mistakes  and you want to use that to inform your  design a lot of times students present  case studies  as a formality because it's a  requirement but that is such a  missed opportunity you are using your  precious minutes of your presentation  time  to talk about someone else's building so  you want to  use that to prepare the viewers for what  they're about to see  with your design so how do you present  case studies so i like to call the  strategy  sam simplify and multiply  so first simplify so presenting  a case study is very different from  doing a case study when you're doing a  case study  you might learn a lot of different  things from it but when you're  presenting it  you just want to show the aspect that  you're going to be employing in your  design  and then you want to diagram graphics  and visual continuity is key  you want to take the plan elevation in  section and you want to diagram on top  of it  and multiply instead of just  studying one or two buildings do  multiple  so i read an article a long time ago  about why architectural research is so  slow to progress compared to other  disciplines like  science or medicine and apparently  it's because we usually tend to  create a unique one-off solution  to the design as opposed to  collecting a massive sample of  architecture  and creating statistics i mean this is  the fundamental tool behind scientific  research  like they take a massive sample and then  they make some assumptions based on that  and so we want to do the same thing we  want to study a bunch of different  buildings  and we want to show hey look like  all of these different buildings achieve  the thing that i want to achieve  by doing it this way and you want to  show that you have that understanding  especially if you want to deviate from  that and come up with your  unique solution i mean if you just show  one or two case studies  no matter how successful that project is  it's not really a good defense for your  case  because you know those buildings could  have had really unique conditions  like they could have had a really unique  site or a program right  so it's not really a good basis  to build your design off of and just one  more thing before we jump into the  example  um the common advice is to study  a building that has the same function as  the function that you want to do  in your project but in my  humble opinion that is not always  necessary  because most architecture is a unique  response to a site or program  and you want to create your unique  response so i wouldn't really base it on  the function or the program  but i would base it on the feeling  or the atmosphere or the way you want  people to behave  in that particular space and i would try  to emulate those  in your design i'll kind of show you  what i mean in a sec  just a little background my thesis  project was called a library  of the mind explorations on slow reading  browsing and serendipity and i was  looking at our culture of speed and  productivity  and how that has affected our culture of  learning this  has also had a pretty big impact in our  building typologies in our libraries  where we went from  quiet reading rooms to large  collaborative working spaces  and so the physical thing that i created  was a library in venice  and through the project it was a  discourse on all these different aspects  like learning  innovation serendipity so this is the  first page of the case study page  and as you can see i'm not showing the  buildings as a main focus of the page  the title of this page is visual harmony  and moment of pause so you can see here  i've given  three examples of buildings that have  achieved visual harmony  through the use of proportions so as you  go through these projects you can give a  little spiel  about the significance of each project  but the idea is that the visuals it  speaks for itself  and um it's very easy  to convey these architectural elements  without  you having to say a bunch of things  about it so just be strategic  based on how much time you're given for  the presentation  if you're only given 15 minutes you kind  of  want to breeze through this if you have  like half an hour  then you can go a little bit more in  depth into each building  and in most cases the case studies that  you'll be doing  are buildings that like your peers  and your professors will know about um  because you are speaking to an  architectural audience  um so like you shouldn't have to  go too much in depth in describing like  the  particular significance of each building  you just want to  distill the qualities of that space that  you're taking into your design  the second one is rhythm and movement  which is important  in slowing us down psychologically and  physically before we enter  our reading space and so i'm  showing how these two examples  are doing that the first one through a  series of arcades  and then in exeter library through a  series of colonnades  and the third one is about nestling  which is um  all about the architecture itself  creating an enclosure  around your body so that you can sit  comfortably and  read your books and then the next one is  narrative sequence which is creating  special  memorable moments in the space through  distinct  architectural elements and then we have  degrees of privacy  slash intimacy  and i'm showing how these three  buildings  uh have created a buffer  between the outside world and  this intimate space of reading both  physically and  psychologically and then the last one is  the design of the stacks  and browsing which i talk about as an  important feature  in creating serendipitous discoveries  and then once you go into showing your  project  you can use the same graphics into your  building so  i'll show you an example here so this is  the pathway  approaching my building so this  labyrinthian pathway  and you get into this narrow low dark  corridor  and bam you come into this open  grand courtyard and you see here i'm  using the same graphics  i was using in my case study and if you  want to reinforce it a little bit more  on the next page you can show  the visuals that you were showing in  your case study on the sides  i typically try to create visual  continuity so i'll have the same  graphics here  and here but i'll just change the color  of the background  just so that like i'm switching back and  forth between my design  and the explanation of my design and you  really have to do this for like  a split second throughout your  presentation just so that  your audience understands okay like i  understand  where that architectural element is  coming from  and then again back to your building  you circulate around the courtyard  through a series of  rhythmic colonnades to slow us down  psychologically and physically before we  get into  the library and then the next page i  have the same  graphics as i had in the case studies  and then on the next page i'll show a  render and i'll just  keep doing this throughout the  presentation  and so as you go through your  presentation you're reinforcing the  reason  why you made certain design moves and at  the end of your presentation  um you can really focus on  um discussing your ideas not like  oh why did you do this like why didn't  you do this  and hopefully it opens up the room for  um a more open discussion rather than  like  a critique on your design and if it is  more of a direct critique then you  are able to speak more intelligibly  about your design moves  um which is really the point you want to  get to  so i hope you guys found this helpful um  and if you have any questions just let  me know  if you enjoy this video i think you will  also enjoy one of my other videos  where i do a quick architectural design  process  from beginning to the end and  in that video you can also see me  attempt to do  a headstand  [Music]  aah  you

